msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:10
msgid "Device hardware glossary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:12
msgid ""
"This section provides concise definitions of hardware components and "
"architectural features of AMD GPUs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:233
msgid "AccVGPR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:235
msgid ""
"Accumulation General Purpose Vector Registers (AccVGPRs) are a special "
"type of :term:`VGPRs <VGPR>` used exclusively for matrix operations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:27
msgid "ALU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:29
msgid ""
"Arithmetic logic units (ALUs) are the primary arithmetic engines that "
"execute mathematical and logical operations within :term:`compute units "
"<Compute units>`. See :ref:`hip:valu` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:17
msgid "AMD device architecture"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:19
msgid ""
"AMD device architecture is based on unified, programmable compute engines"
" known as :term:`compute units (CUs) <Compute units>`. See "
":ref:`hip:hardware_implementation` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:122
msgid "Compute unit versioning"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:124
msgid ""
":term:`Compute units <Compute units>` are versioned with :term:`GFX IP "
"<GFX IP>` identifiers that define their microarchitectural features and "
"instruction set compatibility. See :ref:`hip:gfx_ip` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:22
msgid "Compute units"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:24
msgid ""
"Compute units (CUs) are the fundamental programmable execution engines in"
" AMD GPUs capable of running complex programs. See "
":ref:`hip:compute_unit` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:95
msgid "Data movement engine"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:97
msgid ""
"Data movement engines (DMEs) are specialized hardware units in AMD "
"Instinct MI300 and MI350 series GPUs that accelerate multi-dimensional "
"tensor data copies between global memory and on-chip memory. See "
":ref:`hip:dme` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:242
msgid "GCD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:244
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct MI100 and MI250 series GPUs and AMD Radeon GPUs, the "
"Graphics Compute Die (GCD) contains the GPU's computational elements and "
"lower levels of the cache hierarchy. See :doc:`../../conceptual/gpu-"
"arch/mi250` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:101
msgid "GFX IP"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:103
msgid ""
"GFX IP (Graphics IP) versions are identifiers that specify which "
"instruction formats, memory models, and compute features are supported by"
" each AMD GPU generation. See :ref:`hip:gfx_ip` for versioning "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:107
msgid "GFX IP major version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:109
msgid ""
"The :term:`GFX IP <GFX IP>` major version represents the GPU's core "
"instruction set and architecture. For example, a GFX IP `11` major "
"version corresponds to the RDNA3 architecture, influencing driver support"
" and available compute features. See :ref:`hip:gfx_ip` for versioning "
"information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:114
msgid "GFX IP minor version"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:116
msgid ""
"The :term:`GFX IP <GFX IP>` minor version represents specific variations "
"within a :term:`GFX IP <GFX IP>` major version and affects feature sets, "
"optimizations, and driver behavior. Different GPU models within the same "
"major version can have unique capabilities, impacting performance and "
"supported instructions. See :ref:`hip:gfx_ip` for versioning information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:204
msgid "GPU RAM (VRAM)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:206
msgid ""
"GPU RAM, also known as :term:`global memory <Global memory>` in the HIP "
"programming model, is the large, high-capacity off-chip memory subsystem "
"accessible by all :term:`compute units <Compute units>`, forming the "
"foundation of the device's :ref:`memory hierarchy <hip:hbm>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:186
msgid "Graphics L1 cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:188
msgid ""
"On AMD Radeon GPUs, the read-only graphics level 1 (L1) cache is local to"
" groups of :term:`WGPs <WGP>` called shader arrays, providing fast access"
" to recently used data. AMD Instinct GPUs do not feature the graphics L1 "
"cache."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:198
msgid "Infinity Cache (L3 cache)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:200
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct MI300 and MI350 series GPUs and AMD Radeon GPUs, the "
"Infinity Cache is the last level cache of the cache hierarchy. It is "
"shared by all :term:`compute units <Compute units>` and :term:`WGPs "
"<WGP>` on the GPU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:146
msgid "L0 instruction cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:148
msgid ""
"On AMD Radeon GPUs, the level 0 (L0) instruction cache is local to each "
":term:`WGP <WGP>` and thus shared between the WGP's :term:`compute units "
"<Compute units>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:151
msgid "L0 scalar cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:153
msgid ""
"On AMD Radeon GPUs, the level 0 (L0) scalar data cache is local to each "
":term:`WGP <WGP>` and thus shared between the WGP's :term:`compute units "
"<Compute units>`. It provides the :term:`scalar ALU <SALU>` with fast "
"access to recently used data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:157
msgid "L0 vector cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:159
msgid ""
"On AMD Radeon GPUs, the level 0 (L0) vector data cache is local to each "
":term:`WGP <WGP>` and thus shared between the WGP's :term:`compute units "
"<Compute units>`. It provides the :term:`vector ALU <VALU>` with fast "
"access to recently used data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:163
msgid "L1 instruction cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:165
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct GPUs, the level 1 (L1) instruction cache is local to each"
" :term:`compute unit <Compute units>`. On AMD Radeon GPUs, the L1 "
"instruction cache does not exist as a separate cache level, and "
"instructions are stored in the :term:`L0 instruction cache <L0 "
"instruction cache>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:170
msgid "L1 scalar cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:172
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct GPUs, the level 1 (L1) scalar data cache is local to each"
" :term:`compute unit <Compute units>`, providing the :term:`scalar ALU "
"<SALU>` with fast access to recently used data. On AMD Radeon GPUs, the "
"L1 scalar cache does not exist as a separate cache level, and recently "
"used scalar data is stored in the :term:`L0 scalar cache <L0 scalar "
"cache>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:178
msgid "L1 vector cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:180
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct GPUs, the level 1 (L1) vector data cache is local to each"
" :term:`compute unit <Compute units>`, providing the :term:`vector ALU "
"<VALU>` with fast access to recently used data. On AMD Radeon GPUs, the "
"L1 vector cache does not exist as a separate cache level, and recently "
"used vector data is stored in the :term:`L0 vector cache <L0 vector "
"cache>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:192
msgid "L2 cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:194
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct MI100 series GPUs, the L2 cache is shared across the "
"entire chip, while for all other AMD GPUs the L2 caches are shared by the"
" :term:`compute units <Compute units>` on the same :term:`GCD <GCD>` or "
":term:`XCD <XCD>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:46
msgid "Load/store unit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:48
msgid ""
"Load/store units (LSUs) handle data transfer between :term:`compute units"
" <Compute units>` and the GPU's memory subsystems, managing thousands of "
"concurrent memory operations. See :ref:`hip:lsu` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:210
msgid "Local data share"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:212
msgid ""
"Local data share (LDS) is fast on-chip memory local to each "
":term:`compute unit <Compute units>` and shared among :term:`work-items "
"<Work-item (Thread)>` in a :term:`work-group <Work-group (Block)>`, "
"enabling efficient coordination and data reuse. In the HIP programming "
"model, the LDS is known as shared memory. See :ref:`hip:lds` for LDS "
"programming details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:89
msgid "Matrix cores (MFMA units)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:91
msgid ""
"Matrix cores (MFMA units) are specialized execution units that perform "
"large-scale matrix operations in a single instruction, delivering high "
"throughput for AI and HPC workloads. See :ref:`hip:mfma_units` for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:128
msgid "Register file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:130
msgid ""
"The register file is the primary on-chip memory store in each "
":term:`compute unit <Compute units>`, holding data between arithmetic and"
" memory operations. See :ref:`hip:memory_hierarchy` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:218
msgid "Registers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:220
msgid ""
"Registers are the lowest level of the memory hierarchy, storing per-"
"thread temporary variables and intermediate results. See "
":ref:`hip:memory_hierarchy` for register usage details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:32
msgid "SALU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:34
msgid ""
"Scalar :term:`ALUs <ALU>` (SALUs) operate on a single value per "
":term:`wavefront <Wavefront>` and manage all control flow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:223
msgid "SGPR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:225
msgid ""
"Scalar general-purpose :term:`registers <Registers>` (SGPRs) hold data "
"produced and consumed by a :term:`compute unit <Compute units>`'s "
":term:`scalar ALU <SALU>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:133
msgid "SGPR file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:135
msgid ""
"The :term:`SGPR <SGPR>` file is the :term:`register file <Register file>`"
" that holds data used by the :term:`scalar ALU <SALU>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:83
msgid "SIMD core"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:85
msgid ""
"SIMD cores are execution lanes that perform scalar and vector arithmetic "
"operations inside each :term:`compute unit <Compute unit>`. See "
":ref:`hip:cdna_architecture` and :ref:`hip:rdna_architecture` for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:41
msgid "Special function unit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:43
msgid ""
"Special function units (SFUs) accelerate transcendental and reciprocal "
"mathematical functions such as ``exp``, ``log``, ``sin``, and ``cos``. "
"See :ref:`hip:sfu` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:36
msgid "VALU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:38
msgid ""
"Vector :term:`ALUs <ALU>` (VALUs) perform an arithmetic or logical "
"operation on data for each :term:`work-item <Work-item (Thread)>` in a "
":term:`wavefront <Wavefront>`, enabling data-parallel execution."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:228
msgid "VGPR"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:230
msgid ""
"Vector general-purpose :term:`registers <Registers>` (VGPRs) hold data "
"produced and consumed by a :term:`compute unit <Compute units>`'s "
":term:`vector ALU <VALU>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:138
msgid "VGPR file"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:140
msgid ""
"The :term:`VGPR <VGPR>` file is the :term:`register file <Register file>`"
" that holds data used by the :term:`vector ALU <VALU>`. GPUs with "
":term:`matrix cores <Matrix cores (MFMA units)>` also have :term:`AccVGPR"
" <AccVGPR>` files, used specifically for matrix instructions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:64
msgid "Wavefront (Warp)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:66
msgid ""
"A wavefront (also called a warp) is a group of :term:`work-items <Work-"
"item (Thread)>` that execute in parallel on a single :term:`compute unit "
"<Compute units>`, sharing one instruction stream. See "
":ref:`hip:wavefront` for execution details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:70
msgid "Wavefront scheduler"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:72
msgid ""
"The wavefront scheduler in each :term:`compute unit <Compute units>` "
"decides which :term:`wavefront <wavefront>` to execute each clock cycle, "
"enabling rapid context switching for latency hiding. See :ref:`hip:wave-"
"scheduling` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:76
msgid "Wavefront size"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:78
msgid ""
"The wavefront size is the number of :term:`work-items <Work-item "
"(Thread)>` that execute together in a single :term:`wavefront <Wavefront "
"(Warp)>`. For AMD Instinct GPUs, the wavefront size is 64 threads, while "
"AMD Radeon GPUs have a wavefront size of 32 threads. See "
":ref:`hip:wavefront` for details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:248
msgid "WGP"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:250
msgid ""
"A Workgroup Processor (WGP) is a hardware unit on AMD Radeon GPUs that "
"contains two :term:`compute units <Compute units>` and their associated "
"resources, enabling efficient scheduling and execution of "
":term:`wavefronts <wavefront>`. See :ref:`hip:rdna_architecture` for "
"details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:52
msgid "Work-group (Block)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:54
msgid ""
"A work-group (also called a block) is a collection of :term:`wavefronts "
"<Wavefront (Warp)>` scheduled together on a single :term:`compute unit "
"<Compute units>` that can coordinate through :term:`Local data share "
"<Local data share>` memory. See "
":ref:`hip:inherent_thread_hierarchy_block` for work-group details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:59
msgid "Work-item (Thread)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:61
msgid ""
"A work-item (also called a thread) is the smallest unit of execution on "
"an AMD GPU and represents a single element of work. See :ref:`hip:work-"
"item` for thread hierarchy details."
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:237
msgid "XCD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../reference/glossary/device-hardware.rst:239
msgid ""
"On AMD Instinct MI300 and MI350 series GPUs, the Accelerator Complex Die "
"(XCD) contains the GPU's computational elements and lower levels of the "
"cache hierarchy. See :doc:`../../conceptual/gpu-arch/mi300` for details."
msgstr ""

